Page Refcount Table
===================

Per-physical-frame reference count, used by the COW path in
`vma_handle_fault` and (chunk 6) `fork()`. A user data page that
is mapped from N processes' page tables holds refcount=N; the COW
fault handler bumps refcount on the freshly-allocated copy and
decrements the shared frame back toward 1, eventually returning
the frame to the page pool when the last sharer goes away.

Scope: only **user data pages** are refcounted — i.e. pages
allocated through `kalloc_user_page` and freed through
`kfree_user_page`. The arch's PT/PD/PDPT/PML4 frames and the
kstack slab are allocated directly through `page_alloc` /
`page_free` (raw bump + free-list) and stay outside the
refcount domain. PT pages don't share across processes so they
don't need refcounting; kstacks are permanent slab.

Layout: a flat byte-per-frame array indexed by
`(pa - page_refcnt_base) >> 12`. The base is recorded the first
time the kernel hands out a user data page, by deriving it from
`PAGE_POOL_BASE` (config). Pages outside the tracked range —
kernel image, MMIO, multiboot modules — index out of bounds and
the helpers no-op, treating those frames as permanently allocated.

Saturation: counts max out at 255. A frame exceeding the bound
holds at 255 and silently leaks on later free — acceptable since
255 sharers far exceeds anything a real workload exercises
(MAX_PROCESSES is 32 today). Linux uses 32-bit refcounts; we trade
table size for headroom.

This module is part of the kernel (module oskit.kernel) so it
can be reached from `vma_handle_fault` and the kernel-side mmap
helpers without crossing the `oskit.kernel ↔ oskit.arch` boundary.
The arch's `page_alloc` / `page_free` stay refcount-unaware so the
boundary remains acyclic.

    module oskit.kernel

    import oskit.config.{PAGE_SIZE, PAGE_POOL_BASE}
    import oskit.arch.{page_alloc_zero, page_free}

The pool spans up to 2 GB on aarch64 (PAGE_POOL_END = 0x80000000)
and 256 MB on x86_64 (PAGE_POOL_END = 0x10000000), both starting
at or above `PAGE_POOL_BASE` (0x400000) — x86 pools right there,
aarch64 pools at `_heap_start` (~0x40100000), well above. A 512 KB
bytemap covers 0x400000..0x80400000 for both arches. Sysl array
bounds need a literal, so the `0x80000` is duplicated in the array
decl below — keep it in sync with `PAGE_REFCNT_LEN`.

    val PAGE_REFCNT_LEN = 0x80000

    var page_refcnt: [0x80000]byte
    var page_refcnt_base: u64 = 0
    var page_refcnt_init_done = false

Initialize. Idempotent — a second call is a no-op. Bytemap is
zero-init (BSS) so no memset needed. `base` is the lowest PA the
table will index. Lazy-called from `kalloc_user_page` so we never
have to thread an init call through the boot sequence.

    page_refcnt_init(base: u64)
        if page_refcnt_init_done
            return
        page_refcnt_base = base
        page_refcnt_init_done = true

Compute the bytemap index for a PA, or -1 if out of range. An
uninitialized table reports -1 for every PA.

    page_refcnt_idx(pa: u64) -> int
        if !page_refcnt_init_done
            return -1
        if pa < page_refcnt_base
            return -1
        val off = pa - page_refcnt_base
        if off >= u64(PAGE_REFCNT_LEN) * u64(PAGE_SIZE)
            return -1
        int(off >> 12)

Read the refcount of a frame. Returns 0 for out-of-range
(treated as "not tracked").

    page_refcnt_get(pa: u64) -> int
        val idx = page_refcnt_idx(pa)
        if idx < 0
            return 0
        int(page_refcnt[idx]) & 0xff

Set the refcount of a frame explicitly. Used by `kalloc_user_page`
when handing a fresh frame to the caller.

    page_refcnt_set(pa: u64, n: int)
        val idx = page_refcnt_idx(pa)
        if idx < 0
            return
        page_refcnt[idx] = byte(n)

Increment. Saturating at 255 — a 256th sharer would wrap and
mis-account, so we pin and leak instead.

    page_refcnt_inc(pa: u64)
        val idx = page_refcnt_idx(pa)
        if idx < 0
            return
        val cur = int(page_refcnt[idx]) & 0xff
        if cur < 255
            page_refcnt[idx] = byte(cur + 1)

Decrement. Returns true iff the count hit zero (caller should
release the frame back to the page pool). Refuses to underflow:
calling dec on an already-zero count returns false (no double-free
through this path) and dec on a saturated 255 also returns false
(we don't know the true count, so leak rather than mis-free).
Out-of-range PAs return false too — only refcount-tracked frames
can reach refcount=0 through this path.

    page_refcnt_dec(pa: u64) -> bool
        val idx = page_refcnt_idx(pa)
        if idx < 0
            return false
        val cur = int(page_refcnt[idx]) & 0xff
        if cur == 0
            return false
        if cur == 255
            return false
        page_refcnt[idx] = byte(cur - 1)
        cur == 1


Promote a possibly-untracked frame into the refcount domain by
adding one more sharer. Used by `vma_clone_for_fork` (chunk 6) to
share a parent's existing leaf with a child:

  * If the frame is currently untracked (refcount==0), set it to
    2 — one for the parent's pre-existing mapping plus one for
    the new child. This migrates raw-allocated frames (e.g. the
    chunk-3 allocate-on-write path in `vm_copy_to`) into the
    tracked pool at fork time.
  * If already tracked (refcount>=1), just increment.

The asymmetric init-to-2 keeps the invariant "refcount equals the
number of mappings" so `kfree_user_page` from either side later
correctly decrements without underflow or use-after-free.

    page_refcnt_share(pa: u64)
        val idx = page_refcnt_idx(pa)
        if idx < 0
            return
        val cur = int(page_refcnt[idx]) & 0xff
        if cur == 0
            page_refcnt[idx] = byte(2)
            return
        page_refcnt_inc(pa)


User-data-page allocator. Pulls a zeroed frame from the page pool
and stamps the refcount bytemap to 1. The bytemap base is
`PAGE_POOL_BASE` for both arches; lazy-init on the first call is
just to flip the `page_refcnt_init_done` guard. Returns 0 if the
pool is exhausted.

    kalloc_user_page() -> u64
        if !page_refcnt_init_done
            page_refcnt_init(u64(PAGE_POOL_BASE))
        val pa = page_alloc_zero()
        if pa == u64(0)
            return u64(0)
        page_refcnt_set(pa, 1)
        pa

User-data-page deallocator. The contract is "release one
reference"; how the frame entered the refcount domain governs
what happens:

  * **In refcount range, refcount > 0** — decrement; if it hit
    zero, return the frame to the page pool.
  * **In refcount range, refcount == 0** — frame is in the pool
    span but never went through `kalloc_user_page` (the chunk-3
    allocate-on-write path in `vm_copy_to`, the legacy 0xCC..0xFF
    eager region, etc.). Treat it as untracked: free it
    immediately. Without this fall-through, raw-allocated frames
    that get into a process's user PT (e.g. via the loader's
    `vm_copy_to`) would silently leak when chunk-6's
    `kernel_reap_user_pages` walks the VMA tree on process exit.
  * **Out of refcount range** — kernel image / MMIO / etc. Never
    a pool frame; ignore.

The "refcount==0 means untracked" invariant is preserved by
chunk-5's allocator: `kalloc_user_page` always sets refcount=1
when handing a frame out, and `page_refcnt_dec` only fires from
1→0 on the last drop, which is paired with the `page_free` here.

    kfree_user_page(pa: u64)
        if pa == u64(0)
            return
        val idx = page_refcnt_idx(pa)
        if idx < 0
            return
        val cur = int(page_refcnt[idx]) & 0xff
        if cur == 0
            page_free(pa)
            return
        if page_refcnt_dec(pa)
            page_free(pa)


Selftest -- exercises the refcount module without going through
the page allocator. Used by the chunk-5 test program via a debug
syscall (independent of the COW fault path).

The test uses a synthetic PA at the top of the bytemap so it's
guaranteed in-range without colliding with any frame the page
allocator might hand out. Saves and restores the previous count
so the test is non-destructive.

Returns 0 on success; 1..N on failure at step N.

    page_refcnt_selftest() -> int
        if !page_refcnt_init_done
            page_refcnt_init(u64(PAGE_POOL_BASE))
        val test_pa = page_refcnt_base + u64(PAGE_REFCNT_LEN - 1) * u64(PAGE_SIZE)
        val saved = page_refcnt_get(test_pa)

        page_refcnt_set(test_pa, 1)
        if page_refcnt_get(test_pa) != 1
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 1

        page_refcnt_inc(test_pa)
        if page_refcnt_get(test_pa) != 2
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 2

        if page_refcnt_dec(test_pa)
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 3
        if page_refcnt_get(test_pa) != 1
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 4

        if !page_refcnt_dec(test_pa)
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 5
        if page_refcnt_get(test_pa) != 0
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 6

`dec` on a zero count must report "not freed" (refuses to
underflow).

        if page_refcnt_dec(test_pa)
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 7

Out-of-range PA: get returns 0, dec returns false (untracked).

        val oor = page_refcnt_base + u64(PAGE_REFCNT_LEN) * u64(PAGE_SIZE)
        if page_refcnt_get(oor) != 0
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 8
        if page_refcnt_dec(oor)
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 9

End-to-end through the user-page wrappers: alloc bumps to 1, free
takes it back to 0 and returns the frame to the pool. We can't
easily verify the frame is back on the free list without poking
arch internals, but we can verify the refcount post-conditions.

        val pa = kalloc_user_page()
        if pa == u64(0)
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 10
        if page_refcnt_get(pa) != 1
            kfree_user_page(pa)
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 11
        kfree_user_page(pa)
        if page_refcnt_get(pa) != 0
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 12

`kfree_user_page` on a frame held at refcount>1 must NOT actually
free it — the next sharer will eventually bring it down to 0.

        val pa2 = kalloc_user_page()
        if pa2 == u64(0)
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 13
        page_refcnt_inc(pa2)              // refcount = 2 (synthetic share)
        kfree_user_page(pa2)              // refcount = 1, frame stays
        if page_refcnt_get(pa2) != 1
            kfree_user_page(pa2)
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 14
        kfree_user_page(pa2)              // last ref, frame freed
        if page_refcnt_get(pa2) != 0
            page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
            return 15

        page_refcnt_set(test_pa, saved)
        0
